Backend module boundaries

These are the modules I’d lock in from the start:

Foundation modules
Identity

Users, roles, permissions, branches, auth.

CRM

Customer accounts, contacts, account roles, bill-to logic.

Locations

Service properties, location relationships, access notes, location contacts.

Equipment

Assets, system groupings, equipment status, equipment metadata, install info.

Operations

Jobs, appointments, dispatching, status changes, notes, attachments.

Financial / operational modules
Estimates

Service estimates, replacement estimates, templates, options, approvals.

Billing

Invoices, invoice lines, payments, credits later.

Inventory

Items, stock locations, technician trucks, stock movement.

Purchasing

POs, PO lines, receiving, vendor links.

Job Costing

Labor/material cost rolled up to the job.

Cross-cutting modules
Files

Photos, PDFs, signatures, attachments.

Notifications

Text/email reminders, tech notices, customer messages later.

Audit

Activity timeline, change history, who changed what.

Reporting

KPIs, boards, filters, exports.

How the modules should interact

Keep this rule:

One module owns its own writes. Other modules can read it, but should not directly mutate its tables.

Examples:

Jobs can read location and equipment data.
Billing does not edit jobs; it snapshots job/account/location data when posting invoices.
Inventory does not rewrite jobs; it posts cost entries tied to jobs.
Field app can update equipment through the Equipment module, not by editing location records directly.
Important internal events

These are the glue:

JobCreated
AppointmentScheduled
AppointmentReassigned
TechnicianStatusChanged
JobStatusChanged
EquipmentUpdated
EstimateApproved
InvoicePosted
InventoryIssuedToJob
POReceived
PaymentRecorded

That gives you clean interactions without hard-wiring everything together.

How the office and field apps should communicate
The rule

They do not sync directly with each other.

They both communicate with:

the API
real-time event stream
shared database through backend rules only
Real-time

Use real-time updates for:

dispatch board changes
tech status changes
appointment assignment
job status updates
estimate approval notifications
invoice/payment completion
Offline / sync

The field app needs a local store and sync queue.

Field sync model

Each mobile device keeps:

assigned jobs
appointment info
location details
equipment list
recent service history
notes/photos waiting to upload
pending status changes

When signal returns:

queued actions get pushed
server accepts/rejects each action
updated records get pulled back

That is the right model. Not full two-way chaos.

Good offline rule

The field app can create or edit:

notes
photos
equipment updates
estimate drafts
job status actions
signatures
material usage drafts

But server-side posting should finalize:

invoices
payments
official costing entries
posted financial records

That keeps money clean.

The human-facing visual side
Office app layout

The office app should be dense and practical.

Main navigation
Dashboard
