The most important screen: location page

This should be a core hub.

Location page tabs
Overview
Contacts
Billing / ownership history
Equipment
Service history
Jobs
Invoices
Estimates
Files
Notes
Equipment tab on location page

This must support:

add equipment
edit equipment
deactivate equipment
move/remove old equipment from “active” view
view equipment history
attach photos / install docs
see filter sizes / model / serial / unit location
tie service events to that equipment

Do not hard-delete equipment.
Old units should become inactive or removed-from-service, not disappear.

Dispatch board / daily schedule

This needs to be one of the first major UX designs.

Best layout

Use a three-part layout:

Left panel

Unscheduled / unassigned jobs queue:

job number
customer/location
job type
promised date
priority
tags like no-cool / PM / callback
Center panel

Main timeline board:

rows = technicians
horizontal axis = time of day
appointment cards placed on the timeline
drag/drop reassign and reschedule
visual warning for overlaps / travel / skill mismatch / after-hours

Rows should show:

technician name
truck
current status
skill tags
branch
Right panel

Selected job / appointment detail drawer:

customer
location
contacts
equipment summary
problem description
notes
last service
estimate / invoice status
quick actions
Top toolbar
date selector
day / week toggle
branch filter
technician filters
search
new job
dispatch warnings
print/export later

This layout works because dispatch lives on one screen instead of bouncing around.

Field app visual structure

The field app should feel like a technician’s work console, not a mini office app.

Main tabs
Today
Jobs
Estimates
Inventory
More
Technician workflow on a job

From the job screen, the tech should be able to:

see address and call contact
tap for map/directions
mark on-my-way
mark arrived
view problem description
view service history
open equipment tab
add/edit equipment
add notes and photos
create estimate from template
capture approval/signature
hand off to invoice/payment
mark complete
Equipment editing in the field

Field techs should be able to update:

make/model/serial
filter size
equipment location text
install date
condition/status
notes
photos

More sensitive changes like ownership/billing relationships stay office-side.

The build order

Do not start with “everything.”

Build this in layers.

Layer 1: product blueprint

Roles, workflows, screen map, dispatch board layout, mobile flow, module boundaries.

Layer 2: foundation

Auth, users, branches, audit, attachments, shared UI shells, logging, seed data.

Layer 3: CRM and location core

Accounts, locations, contacts, equipment, history model.

Layer 4: operations

Jobs, appointments, dispatch, notes, photos, service history.

Layer 5: field mobile

Job list, job details, status flow, offline sync, equipment editing.

Layer 6: estimates and invoices

Estimate builder, templates, invoice generation, signatures, payments.

Layer 7: inventory and POs

Truck stock, PO receiving, issue to job, job costing.

Layer 8: reporting and admin

Permissions, dashboards, exports, audit views.

Layer 9: customer-facing extras

Online booking, customer portal, reminders, maybe later membership/service agreement flows.

Month-by-month roadmap

This assumes a serious internal build, not a weekend side project.

Month 1 — Blueprint and product definition

Goal: decide exactly what is being built before schema or coding explodes.

Deliverables:

