End-of-month demo:

stable internal v1 with real users
What gets postponed on purpose

Do not try to do these early:

customer self-booking
customer portal
financing workflows
recurring service agreements
advanced payroll
commission engine
marketing attribution
multi-tenant SaaS architecture
deep accounting integrations

Those come later.

The reason is simple: your schedule logic, field workflow, invoicing, and inventory must work first or the rest sits on sand.

The exact structural rule to follow

For the whole project, use this order:

First define
who uses it
what screens exist
how jobs move through the system
how office and field communicate
what must be real-time
what must work offline
what modules own what
Then define
API contracts
state machines
events
data ownership
Then define
database tables
indexes
snapshots
history rules

That order is what keeps this from turning into a giant unfinished database with no usable product.

My recommendation for the code and product stack

For this project, I would lock in:

Office app: Next.js web app
Field app: React Native with Expo
Backend: NestJS
Database: PostgreSQL
Cache/realtime/pubsub: Redis
File storage: S3-compatible object storage
Mobile local storage: SQLite on device
Real-time: WebSocket layer from backend
Shared contracts: TypeScript shared types/validation

That gives you one language across the whole stack, faster iteration, shared validation, and easier AI-assisted development.
